A.task('practical_task',['4.3.5-04','4.3.5-05'],'''A learner investigated the effect of temperature on the germination of seeds. At each temperature she set up two separate samples of ten seeds taken from one batch. After five days she counted the seeds that had germinated, where germination means that a root at least 2 mm long has emerged. Her results are shown in the table. Describe and explain the results by answering parts (a) to (g).

| Temperature / °C | Germinated out of 10, sample 1 | Germinated out of 10, sample 2 | Mean germination / % |
|---|---|---|---|
| 15 | 3 | 4 | 35 |
| 20 | 8 | 9 | ... |
| 25 | 10 | 9 | 95 |
| 35 | 4 | 5 | 45 |

(a) State the independent variable. [1]
(b) State what was counted and the rule used to decide whether a seed had germinated. [1]
(c) Calculate the missing mean percentage at 20 °C. Show your working. [2]
(d) Describe the pattern in the results and identify the temperature with the highest germination. [2]
(e) State one variable, other than temperature, that should be kept the same. [1]
(f) Some samples at 35 °C dried out. Suggest why this is an error, and an improvement. [2]
(g) Suggest why 25 °C cannot be called the best temperature for germination from these results. [1]''',
 '''(a) Temperature.
(b) The number of seeds with an emerged root at least 2 mm long after five days.
(c) 8 + 9 = 17; 17 ÷ 20 = 0.85; 0.85 × 100 = 85, giving 85%.
(d) Germination increases from 15 °C to 25 °C, then falls at 35 °C; 25 °C gives the highest germination, 95%.
(e) Any one, such as the volume of water (moisture) or the light; the same seed batch or the same time is also accepted.
(f) Drying changes the water supply as well as the temperature, so the fall at 35 °C may be partly caused by lack of water. Check the dishes and add water to keep the moisture equal, without waterlogging.
(g) Temperatures between those tested, such as 30 °C, were not tested, so the best temperature could lie between them.''',
 ['(a) 1 mark for naming temperature as the variable that was changed. (b) 1 mark for the number of seeds germinated together with the 2 mm root rule.',
 '(c) 1 mark for the method from the table data: (8 + 9) ÷ 20 × 100. 1 mark for the answer 85%. (d) 1 mark for the trend: germination rises up to 25 °C, then falls at 35 °C. 1 mark for identifying 25 °C as the temperature with the highest germination.',
 '(e) 1 mark for one controlled variable, such as moisture or light. (f) 1 mark for the error: drying changes the water supply as well as the temperature. 1 mark for the improvement: keep the moisture equal in every dish.',
 '(g) 1 mark for the limitation: temperatures between those tested, such as 30 °C, were not tested. The total is 10 marks.'],10,['AO3'],command='describe and explain')

summaries={
 '4.1':'| Division | Chromosome outcome | Genetic result and role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mitosis | number kept the same | identical nuclei; growth, repair, replacement |\n| Meiosis | diploid to haploid | different combinations of chromosomes; makes gametes |\n\nMitosis copies the chromosomes and shares the copies equally. Meiosis halves the number, so that fertilisation can restore it. Use the chromosome count, and not the size of the cells, to tell the two divisions apart. A root-tip squash, stained and spread thinly, shows dividing cells, and a good drawing records only what is seen.',
 '4.2.1':'Asexual reproduction uses one parent without gametes and gives genetically identical offspring. Sexual reproduction fuses the nuclei of two gametes to form a zygote, and gives offspring that differ from each other. Fast multiplication and variation have different advantages in different conditions. Binary fission in bacteria, spores in fungi and tubers in potatoes are three examples of asexual reproduction.',
 '4.2.2':'| Event | Material and site |\n|---|---|\n| Pollination | pollen moves from anther to stigma |\n| Fertilisation | male and female nuclei fuse in the ovule |\n| Dispersal | seeds or fruits move away from the parent |\n\nSelf-pollination stays within one plant, and cross-pollination reaches a different plant of the same species. Insect-pollinated and wind-pollinated flowers have different adaptations. A pollen tube carries the male nucleus to the ovule. Dispersal reduces competition and helps a species to spread.',
 '4.3.1':'| Hormone | Main role in the cycle |\n|---|---|\n| FSH | makes a follicle develop, and the follicle makes oestrogen |\n| LH | surge causes ovulation and the formation of the corpus luteum |\n| Oestrogen | builds the uterus lining; high level stimulates the LH surge |\n| Progesterone | maintains the lining after ovulation |\n\nThe ovaries make oestrogen and progesterone in the cycle, and the placenta makes them in pregnancy. The place where a hormone is made is not the same as the target where it acts.',
 '4.3.2':'The sperm and egg nuclei fuse in an oviduct, and the embryo implants in the uterus. The placenta exchanges oxygen, nutrients, carbon dioxide and urea between separate maternal and fetal blood supplies, and the cord carries the fetal blood. Early development increases complexity, and later development increases size. In labour the uterus contracts, the cervix widens, the baby is delivered and the afterbirth follows. Breast milk and formula milk each have advantages and disadvantages.',
 '4.3.3':'Birth control works by avoiding intercourse, by fertility awareness, by hormones, by barriers or by cutting reproductive tubes. A copper IUD harms sperm and releases no hormone. Fertility treatment can stimulate ovulation, but it cannot overcome every cause of infertility. Access, cost, consent and beliefs affect how people decide, and a health worker can give advice on an individual choice.',
 '4.3.4':'HIV is passed on by infected body fluids, through sex, shared needles, blood and from mother to child, and not by everyday contact. Prevention blocks each route. Untreated HIV weakens the immune system, so other infections, such as TB, become more severe. Treatment and support reduce the effects on health and on families and the economy. HIV is the virus, and AIDS is the advanced stage.',
 '4.3.5':'Growth is an increase in dry mass and size, and development is an increase in complexity. Germination needs water, a suitable temperature and oxygen. Percentage germination = number germinated ÷ number tested × 100. Choose height, fresh mass or dry mass to suit the question, since water content and harvesting affect the measurement. A growth curve is drawn from recorded values of time and mass.',
 '4.4':'Genetic information is passed from one generation to the next through the gametes. Features acquired during life, such as damage, are not inherited, and the environment can change the phenotype without changing an allele.',
 '4.4.1':'| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Chromosome | a thread-like DNA structure carrying genes |\n| Gene | a length of DNA that codes for a protein |\n| Allele | a version of a gene |\n| Haploid, diploid | one set, two sets of chromosomes |\n\nHomologous chromosomes carry the same genes at the same loci, and may carry the same or different alleles. The order of bases decides the order of amino acids, and proteins control cell function.',
 '4.4.2':'The genotype is the combination of alleles and the phenotype is the observable feature. Homozygous means two identical alleles and heterozygous means two different ones. A genetic diagram shows the parents, the gametes, the offspring and the ratios. Use a clear allele key for complete dominance, codominance and X-linked inheritance. A test cross with a homozygous recessive individual can show an unknown genotype. A ratio predicts proportions and not exact numbers.',
 '4.4.3':'Continuous variation has a range with intermediate values, whereas discontinuous variation has separate categories. Both genes and the environment affect the phenotype. A gene mutation is a change in the base sequence of DNA, whereas Down’s syndrome is a change in chromosome number. Where malaria occurs, heterozygotes for the sickle-cell allele survive better, which keeps the allele in the population despite the disease it causes.',
 '4.4.4':'Natural selection needs inherited variation and a selection pressure that causes unequal reproduction. Alleles become more or less common over generations, and individuals do not choose the changes they need. Hydrophytes and xerophytes have features that suit water or dry conditions. Antibiotic resistance is an example of selection of inherited variants. Artificial selection uses breeders’ choices over many generations to produce useful varieties.'}
